It's using pmatch (as shown in the screenshot linked in earlier 
article). I took the ../include/config.h entries:
# ifndef __APPLE__
#  define USER_SOUNDS_REGEX
# endif

As a 'you ain't gonna get regex on Macs' :-)

The sound settings in my ~/.nethackrc (were - I've played 
replacing spaces with tabs & such) identical to what I used 
successfully with the downloaded binary of Qt 3.4.2 for Mac OS X:

#sound settings for things that happen in the dungeon
SOUNDDIR=/Users/Martin/nethacksounds
#message to match, sound file to play, volume (does that work on 
Mac OS??)
SOUND=MESG "*needs food, badl*"	"weak.aiff"	50
SOUND=MESG "*feel wea*"	"weak.aiff"	50
SOUND=MESG "*bear trap close*"	"beartrap.aiff"	50
SOUND=MESG "*board beneath you squeak*"	"squeek.aiff"	50
SOUND=MESG "*board beneath .....* squeak*"	"squeek.aiff"	60
SOUND=MESG "*to NetHac*"	"awaits.aiff"	3.0

[I've trimmed the list substantially...]
With the last one shown uncommented /every/ string that matches 
produces 'awaits.aiff', as it matches the welcome string, so is 
the first to play. If I comment it out, then I usually end up with 
the sound of hitting something, missing something, or a door 
opening as my one-and-only sound effect.

Removing the leading * from the match-string yields complete 
silence.

